  "historical_output_text": " Cell division is a fundamental process by which living organisms grow, develop, and repair themselves. This process is crucial for maintaining the health and function of all living organisms. At its core, cell division involves the replication of genetic material and the subsequent distribution of that material into two daughter cells.\n\nThe process of cell division can be broken down into two main phases: interphase and mitosis. Interphase is the period when the cell is not dividing but is actively growing and carrying out its normal functions. During this phase, the cell's DNA is replicated, resulting in two identical sets of chromosomes. This replication ensures that each daughter cell will receive a complete copy of the genetic material.\n\nThe second phase, mitosis, is the actual process of cell division. It can be further divided into four stages: prophase, metaphase, anaphase, and tel",
